VERDICT: UNSUPPORTED -- there is no RIP-to-BFD binding on this platform. BFD
itself is present, startable and fully functional on the same link between the
same two devices; what the case asks for, BFD *with* RIP, does not exist in the
CLI, in any mode, in either RIP version, with both daemons running.
      is BFD absent or licence-gated? ... NO. `service bfd` starts bfdd; a
                                          `bfd peer` session comes up and runs
                                          on the wire with no routing protocol
                                          configured at all
      is the RIP-to-BFD binding there? .. NO. Five independent CLI probes, the
                                          vendor's own RIP guide, and an
                                          `ip ospf bfd` contrast on the SAME
                                          interface in the same minute
      does BFD work here? ............... YES. 3 x 300 ms negotiated, read on the
                                          wire, and it detected a silent
                                          neighbour failure in ~1.4 s (RIP v2)
                                          and ~1.1 s (RIP v1)
      what the gap costs ................ RIP kept the dead prefix in the FIB and
                                          called it "best" for ~171.6 s while the
                                          dataplane measured 0/5 on both subjects
                                          with its in-leg instrument at 5/5
      reproduced under both versions .... v1 and v2 each read off the wire, each
                                          forwarding 5/5 before the failure, the
                                          binding absent in both

NEEDS:   A binding between RIP and BFD, on both ends of the adjacency: a command
         that makes a RIP neighbour relationship subscribe to a BFD session, so
         that a BFD "control detection time expired" withdraws the RIP routes
         learned through that neighbour instead of leaving them to RIP's own
         ~180 s route timeout. On AlliedWare Plus that would be an `ip rip bfd`
         (or equivalent) in interface mode, or a `bfd` sub-command under
         `router rip` / `neighbor <addr>`, mirroring the `ip ospf bfd` that this
         same build offers on this same interface.

ABSENT:  Five CLI probes, every one of them taken with BOTH daemons already
         running (bfdd pid 4391, ripd pid 4416 -- see STEP 1), so none of this is
         the service gate and none of it is a licence. All five were taken with
         `tools/qmark.py`, which types `PREFIX ?` with NO carriage return and
         clears the line with Ctrl-U, and which self-tested before each probe
         ("self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U then CR executed nothing").
         Verbatim:

         (1) global config mode offers BFD, standalone, and nothing under `ip`:
             awplus(config)# bfd ?
               echo     Echo mode
               peer     Configure a BFD peer
               profile  Create a profile
             awplus(config)# ip ?   -> 32 completions, NONE of them `bfd`

         (2) `config-router` mode for RIP refuses the word outright:
             awplus(config)# router rip
             awplus(config-router)# bfd ?
             bfd ?
             % Unrecognized command

         (3) the COMPLETE `router rip` command list -- 24 commands, no `bfd`:
             awplus(config-router)# ?
             Router configuration commands:
               address-family         Enter Address Family command mode
               alliedware-behavior    Configure AlliedWare behavior
               cisco-metric-behavior  Set metric updation behavior as Cisco
               default-information    Control distribution of default route
               default-metric         Set a metric of redistribute routes
               distance               Administrative distance
               distribute-list        Filter networks in routing updates
               do                     To run exec commands in config mode
               exit                   End current mode and down to previous mode
               fullupdate             Enable full route table transmission on trig. updates
               help                   Description of the interactive help system
               maximum-prefix         Set the maximum number of RIP routes
               neighbor               Specify a neighbor router
               network                Enable routing on an IP network
               no                     Negate a command or set its defaults
               offset-list            Modify RIP metric
               passive-interface      Suppress routing updates on an interface
               recv-buffer-size       Set the RIP UDP receive buffer size
               redistribute           Redistribute information from another routing
                                      protocol
               route                  Static route advertisement (debugging purpose)
               show                   Show running system information
               timers                 Adjust routing timers
               version                Set routing protocol version

         (4) interface mode, on vlan61 -- the very interface RIP is running on:
             awplus(config)# interface vlan61
             awplus(config-if)# ip rip ?
               authentication  Authentication control
               receive         Advertisement reception
               receive-packet  Enable receiving packet through the specified interface
               send            Advertisement transmission
               send-packet     Enable sending packets through the specified interface
               split-horizon   Perform split horizon
             -- six completions, no `bfd`. And:
             awplus(config-if)# bfd ?
             bfd ?
             % Unrecognized command
             awplus(config-if)# ip ?  -> 20 completions, NONE of them `bfd`

         (5) the per-neighbour form, which is where a binding would hang if it
             were not per-interface:
             awplus(config-router)# neighbor 10.0.61.2 ?
             neighbor 10.0.61.2 ?
               <cr>
             -- `<cr>` only. There is no suffix of any kind.

         THE SAME-PARSER CONTRAST, on the same interface, 21 seconds after probe
         (4), which is what makes this an A/B and not merely an absence:
             awplus(config-if)# ip ospf ?
               A.B.C.D              Address of interface
               authentication       Enable authentication
               authentication-key   Authentication password (key)
               bfd                  Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD)
               cost                 Interface cost
               ... (14 more)
         `ip ospf bfd` IS accepted on vlan61 on this build. `ip rip bfd` is not.

         THE VENDOR'S OWN DOCUMENTATION AGREES. The RIP Feature Overview and
         Configuration Guide extracts to 1045 lines of text and contains ZERO
         matches for "bfd" or "bidirectional", case-insensitive. The product's
         RIP document never mentions BFD at all.

CAVEAT:  What this run did NOT establish. BFD was exercised only as a standalone
         single-hop `bfd peer` over one VLAN interface with the negotiated
         defaults (detect-multiplier 3, RX/TX 300 ms); echo mode, `bfd profile`
         and `multihop` exist in the parser and were not exercised. `ip ospf bfd`
         was shown to EXIST on this interface; no OSPF session was brought up, so
         this run does not claim OSPF-over-BFD works, only that the binding RIP
         lacks is one the same parser offers to OSPF. The absence was established
         from the shipped CLI surface and the RIP guide; no release note or
         roadmap was consulted. The ~171.6 s figure is RIP's own route timeout on
         one pair of routers with default timers, counted from the last update
         actually received (which arrived ~8 s before the stimulus), so it is a
         measurement of this configuration, not a universal constant. Only IPv4
         RIP v1 and v2 were covered, which is what the case title names; RIPng
         was not touched.

