  Method: no claim here rests on a `show` command alone. Each protocol is read
  off the wire (BFD by UDP/3784 + TTL 255 + 52-byte payload; RIP by its decoded
  version field), and each reachability claim is a counted arpleg leg carrying a
  CONTROL that must stay at 0 and an INSTRUMENT that must stay at 5, in the same
  capture as the subject, so a zero on a subject is a measurement and not a dead
  capture. Every counted leg used identical arguments but for `--tag`:

    tools/tb sudotool tb277 ckt1406d arpleg --tag <L> \
      --out /tmp/ckt1406d/T47201 --iface eth3 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 \
      --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 \
      --icmp 192.168.70.1,192.168.71.1,203.0.113.9,10.38.22.34 \
      --count 5 --filter ip

STEP 0 -- baseline (feature OFF): neither daemon is running, and what each refusal says  done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# show bfd peer
% BFD protocol daemon is not running
awplus# show bfd peer brief
% BFD protocol daemon is not running
awplus# show bfd session
^
% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.   <-- `show bfd session` DOES NOT EXIST on
                                              this build; the verb is `show bfd
                                              peer`. One keystroke away from being
                                              misread as "BFD is absent"
awplus# show ip rip
% RIP protocol daemon is not running or feature license is not available
awplus# show ip rip database
% RIP protocol daemon is not running or feature license is not available
awplus# show running-config | include service
service ssh
no service telnet
service http
no service dhcp-server                    <-- no `service bfd`, no `service rip`
awplus# show process | include bfd
                                          <-- no output: no bfd process
awplus# show process | include rip
                                          <-- no output: no rip process

  The two refusals are not the same sentence, and the difference is information:
  the BFD one carries NO licence clause ("% BFD protocol daemon is not running")
  while the RIP one does ("...or feature license is not available"). So BFD on
  this platform is service-gated ONLY -- there is no BFD licence bit that could
  be missing, which is consistent with `show license`.

  => STEP 0 done: the measured starting state is both protocols OFF, with no
     configuration of either on the DUT, and the "off" refusals recorded verbatim
     so that everything counted later is new. The baseline also disposes of the
     first wrong reading of this case before it can be made: BFD's absence here
     is a stopped daemon, not a missing licence.

