  => STEP 2 UNSUPPORTED: the platform has no way to attach BFD to RIP. The word
     is refused outright in both the mode where RIP's interface knobs live and
     the mode where RIP itself is configured; the complete command lists of both
     modes are printed above and neither holds it; the per-neighbour form takes
     no suffix at all; and the vendor's own RIP guide never mentions BFD. This is
     not an absence read off one failed command: `ip ospf bfd` is offered by the
     same parser on the same interface in the same minute, so the capability
     exists in this image and is simply not wired to RIP. Both daemons were
     running throughout, so it is neither the service gate nor a licence.

STEP 3 -- RIP v2 forwards: proven in the dataplane, with an A/B turned by one command  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: each router owns a loopback prefix the other can reach only through
  RIP. One prefix, 192.168.71.0/24, is deliberately CONFIGURED on swi_b and NOT
  advertised, so the "off" measurement lives inside the same capture as the "on"
  one and the A/B is closed later by a single `network` statement.

awplus(config)# interface lo
awplus(config-if)# ip address 192.168.80.1/24
awplus(config)# router rip
awplus(config-router)# network 10.0.61.0/30
awplus(config-router)# network 192.168.80.0/24

x960-28YSQ(config)# interface lo
x960-28YSQ(config-if)# ip address 192.168.70.1/24
x960-28YSQ(config-if)# ip address 192.168.71.1/24 secondary
x960-28YSQ(config)# router rip
x960-28YSQ(config-router)# network 10.0.61.0/30
x960-28YSQ(config-router)# network 192.168.70.0/24

  The two units differ, and the two `.cfg` files differ for this reason. On the
  DUT's `lo`, which had no primary IPv4 address:

awplus(config-if)# ip address 192.168.80.1/24 secondary
% Configure primary first lo

  so the DUT's loopback took the address WITHOUT `secondary`, while swi_b's `lo`
  already had a primary and its second address needed `secondary`.

  Adjacency, 20 s after `router rip`:

awplus# show ip rip
  Rc  10.0.61.0/30                 1          vlan61
  R   192.168.70.0/24  10.0.61.2   2  10.0.61.2  vlan61  02:45
  Rc  192.168.80.0/24              1          lo
awplus# show ip route rip
  R  192.168.70.0/24 [120/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:00:45
awplus# show ip rip interface vlan61
  vlan61 is up, line protocol is up
