    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL, no route)   : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    10.38.22.34  (INSTRUMENT, DUT SVI) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies -- alive

  FAR-SIDE ATTRIBUTION: the replies for 192.168.70.1 carry Ethernet source
  00:00:cd:42:30:29, which is swi_b's own MAC. The frames really crossed the DUT
  and were answered by the other router.

  THE A/B, CLOSED BY ONE COMMAND:

x960-28YSQ(config-router)# network 192.168.71.0/24        (05:32:51 UTC)
awplus# show ip route rip                                 (12 s later)
  R  192.168.71.0/24 [120/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:00:12
                                          <-- a triggered update, not a 30 s
                                              periodic one

  COUNTED LEG L2, nothing else changed:
    192.168.70.1  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies      (unchanged)
    192.168.71.1  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies      ** 0/5 -> 5/5 **
    203.0.113.9   : 5 sent, 0 echo replies      (control still zero)
    10.38.22.34   : 5 sent, 5 echo replies      (instrument still alive)

  => STEP 3 PASS: RIP v2 is genuinely routing on this bench, proven in the
     dataplane and not in a routing table -- a prefix that was unreachable became
     reachable because one `network` statement was typed on the far router, while
     the no-route control stayed at zero and the instrument stayed at five in the
     same captures. That makes every later zero on these subjects a measurement.

STEP 4 -- BFD works on this platform, and it is running on the wire       PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: BFD is configured with NO routing protocol binding of any kind (there
  is none to make -- STEP 2), as a bare `bfd peer` on each end.

awplus(config)# bfd peer 10.0.61.2 interface vlan61
awplus(config-bfd-peer)# end
awplus# show bfd peer
BFD Peers:
    peer 10.0.61.2 vrf default interface vlan61
        ID: 2691434186
        Remote ID: 0
        Active mode
        Status: down                      <-- far end not configured yet
        ...
        Local timers:
            Detect-multiplier: 3
            Receive interval: 300ms
            Transmission interval: 300ms
            Echo receive interval: 50ms
            Echo transmission interval: disabled

x960-28YSQ(config)# bfd peer 10.0.61.1 interface vlan61
x960-28YSQ# show bfd peer brief
Session count: 1
SessionId  LocalAddress  PeerAddress  Status
1702072268 10.0.61.2     10.0.61.1    up

awplus# show bfd peer
    peer 10.0.61.2 vrf default interface vlan61
        ID: 2691434186      Remote ID: 1702072268
        Status: up          Uptime: 17 second(s)
        Local timers : Detect-multiplier 3, RX 300ms, TX 300ms
        Remote timers: Detect-multiplier 3, RX 300ms, TX 300ms
                                          <-- negotiated detection time
                                              = 3 x 300 ms = 900 ms

  ON THE WIRE, read with the DUT's own tcpdump (vlan61 has no host NIC, so
  nothing else can see this segment):

awplus# tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 4 -nn -v udp port 3784
05:34:23.074407 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, id 12857, flags [DF], proto UDP, length 52)
    10.0.61.1.49152 > 10.0.61.2.3784:
05:34:23.314464 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, ... length 52)
    10.0.61.1.49152 > 10.0.61.2.3784:
05:34:23.327195 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, ... length 52)
    10.0.61.2.49152 > 10.0.61.1.3784:
05:34:23.567202 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, ... length 52)
    10.0.61.2.49152 > 10.0.61.1.3784:
4 packets captured

  - bidirectional UDP/3784, TTL 255, TOS 0xc0 (CS6), 52-byte payload: BFD control.
  - spacing 240 ms in BOTH directions = the negotiated 300 ms with RFC 5880
    jitter (300 x 0.8).
  - this build's tcpdump mislabels the BFD payload "BCM-LI-SHIM ... (invalid)".
    The port, the TTL 255 and the length are what identify it; the decoder line
    is a dissector artefact and must not be read as a malformed packet.

  => STEP 4 PASS: BFD is present, startable, configurable with no routing
     protocol at all, and genuinely running between these two devices at the
     negotiated interval -- confirmed from both ends' session state AND from the
     packets themselves. The UNSUPPORTED verdict of this case is therefore about
     the RIP binding alone and cannot be read as "BFD is broken here".

STEP 5 -- the consequence: BFD detects in ~1.4 s, RIP in ~171.6 s, and the dataplane blackholes between  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a SILENT NEIGHBOUR FAILURE -- `shutdown` on swi_b's vlan61 SVI. The
  physical port and the DUT's own vlan61 stay UP, so the DUT cannot learn of the
  failure from interface state. Its only recourses are BFD and RIP's own timers,
  and nothing connects the one to the other. Instrument: `tools/poll.py` on the
  DUT, 3 s interval for 250 s, sampling `show bfd peer brief` and
  `show ip route rip`, every sample epoch-stamped by the BOX.

    tools/tb poll tb277 ckt1406d u2 115200 3 250 \
        /tmp/ckt1406d/T47201/poll1.done "show bfd peer brief" "show ip route rip"

    event                                        box epoch        offset
    -------------------------------------------- ---------------  ---------
    `shutdown` echoed on swi_b                   1792128922.638   t0
    last DUT sample reading BFD **up**           1792128922.022   t0 - 0.6 s
    first DUT sample reading BFD **down**        1792128925.022   t0 + 2.4 s
    last sample with 192.168.70.0/24 in the FIB  1792129091.237   t0 + 168.6 s
    first sample with it GONE from the FIB       1792129094.238   t0 + 171.6 s

  BFD's own counter pins the detection finer than the 3 s poll can. Read at
  05:39:50 UTC (box epoch 1792129190):

awplus# show bfd peer
    Status: down
    Downtime: 4 minute(s), 26 second(s)   <-- transition at epoch 1792128924
    Diagnostics: control detection time expired

  1792128924 - 1792128922.638 = **1.4 s**, consistent with the 900 ms detect time
  plus the time for `shutdown` to take effect on the far device. And the
  diagnostic says the session died of its OWN detect timer, not of an
  administrative down -- so the 1.4 s is BFD DETECTING, not BFD being switched
  off.

      ** BFD: ~1.4 s.   RIP: ~171.6 s.   A factor of about 120. **

  After the RIP timeout:

awplus# show ip route rip
No entries in route table
awplus# show ip rip database
  R  192.168.70.0/24  10.0.61.2  16  10.0.61.2  vlan61  00:18
  R  192.168.71.0/24  10.0.61.2  16  10.0.61.2  vlan61  00:18
                                          <-- the FIB is clean and both prefixes
                                              sit at metric 16 in the
                                              garbage-collect state

  THE BLACK HOLE, MEASURED IN THE DATAPLANE. The poll proves the two timers are
  different; it does not by itself prove traffic is lost in between. So the
  stimulus was repeated and a counted leg taken INSIDE the window:

  swi_b vlan61 `no shutdown` (epoch 1792129250.9) -> BFD up, both routes back in 12 s
  swi_b vlan61 `shutdown`    (epoch 1792129287.1) = t0

cap L3-blackhole-eth3.pcap: 27 frames
    192.168.70.1 (SUBJECT)    : 5 sent, **0** echo replies     (was 5/5)
    192.168.71.1 (SUBJECT)    : 5 sent, **0** echo replies     (was 5/5)
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    10.38.22.34  (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, **5** echo replies  -- the capture is
                                alive, so the two zeros are measurements

  DUT state at box epoch 1792129322, i.e. t0 + 35 s:

awplus# show bfd peer brief
2691434186 10.0.61.1  10.0.61.2  down
awplus# show ip route rip
  R  192.168.70.0/24 [120/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:01:07   <-- STILL THERE
  R  192.168.71.0/24 [120/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:01:07
awplus# show ip route 192.168.70.1
Routing entry for 192.168.70.0/24
  Known via "rip", distance 120, metric 2, **best**
  Last update 00:01:08 ago
  * via 10.0.61.2, vlan61

  => STEP 5 PASS: three instruments read at one instant say three different
     things -- BFD says the path is dead, the routing table says the prefix is
     reachable and calls it "best", and the dataplane is dropping every frame to
     it while the instrument flow in the same capture arrives 5 of 5. BFD had
     known for ~34 seconds and RIP did not act on it, because on this platform
     there is nothing to carry that knowledge across. This is the operational
     cost of the gap STEP 2 proved, measured end to end rather than argued.

